  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nWhile most of the attention to the weather has been focused on \nthe potent storm system over the northeastern CONUS, our area \nhas continued to run fairly tranquil with seasonable \ntemperatures and persistent dry weather. This may change today \nas a weak surface trough sagging southward from a low pressure \ncenter over southern Manitoba province crosses the Dakotas this \nmorning and across the Upper Midwest through tonight. This is \nevident by the swath of clouds over the western Dakotas early \nthis morning, while the expansive storm system over the \nnortheastern CONUS is spreading cloud cover as far west as \nMI/WI. Both systems will slowly shift east today, resulting in \nan increase in cloud cover as the day progresses from the \napproaching trough. Short-range models still indicate a narrow \nline of precipitation as the trough pushes east from the Dakotas\ninto MN today and into WI this evening. Very little in the way \nof precipitation will be realized at the surface, from a trace \nto a hundredth or two of liquid measure. The concern is the type\nof precip that reaches the ground. Should a deep enough dry \nlayer present itself, the precip could come in the form of \ndrizzle, or more to the point, freezing drizzle due to air \ntemperatures running below freezing until midday today. That \nsaid, the expectation is a brief mix of rain/snow showers as the\nline of precipitation passes across the area. The p-type is \nexpected to be mainly rain from midday through sunset with the \nabove freezing temperatures, then as the temperatures drop below\nfreezing, and the precipitation mainly over western WI at this \npoint, some rain/snow showers are possible in western WI before \nthe precipitation altogether shifts out of the WFO MPX coverage \narea by about midnight. So, again, very little to potentially \neven no precipitation is expected to be measured by some wet \nroadways could result in slick travel conditions this morning in\nwestern MN and this evening in western WI.\n\nQuiet weather conditions return starting on Saturday and lasting \ngenerally through to late next week, and this is despite the \npassage of a cold front through the area on Sunday. This front \nis expected to be dry, but there will be a bit of a temperature \ndrop for Sunday and Monday with and behind the frontal passage. \nWhile highs today and Saturday run in the low-mid 40s, this will\nbe followed by highs only in the 30s Sunday-Monday. These \nvalues would be nearly to slightly below normal. However, these \nvalues won't persist as southerly flow returns starting Tuesday,\nbringing temperatures back to above normal levels for the start\nof December. There are some indications of a frontal passage \nnear the end of next week which could bring precipitation \nchances to most of our coverage area. However, the system does \nnot look impactful and looks to have rain as the prevailing \nprecipitation type with some snow showers mixed in. Thus, our \nfairly benign weather trend will move from November into \nDecember.",
